---
import Card from "astro-theme-university/components/Card.astro";
import CardGrid from "astro-theme-university/components/CardGrid.astro";
import ContentLayout from "astro-theme-university/layouts/ContentLayout.astro";
import heroImage from "../assets/images/hero-home.png";
import { courseMeta } from "../course-config";
import { sessionLabels, siteConfig } from "../site-config";
---

<ContentLayout
  title={`${courseMeta.code}: ${courseMeta.title}`}
  description={courseMeta.description}
  heroImage={heroImage}
  heroImageAlt="Two people under a windblown tree, one leaning on a low stone wall with a notebook and one sitting on the grass mid-sentence, with loops, question marks and scribbled shapes drifting off on the wind. Flat gold and black on cream."
  {...siteConfig}
>
  <ul class="course-tags" aria-label="Course tags">
    {courseMeta.tags.map((tag) => <li>{tag}</li>)}
  </ul>

  <h2>Unfinished thinking</h2>
  <p>
    A thought that doesn't get to finish doesn't go away. It goes back in the
    queue. This course argues the trouble was never volume. You're not
    thinking <em>too much</em>. You're thinking in a loop that never reaches
    an end, so it starts again. That changes the question. It stops being how
    to think less, and becomes what a thought needs in order to finish.
  </p>
  <p>
    Those conditions turn out to be oddly specific, and mostly unavailable.
    That's what the semester is for.
  </p>
  <p>
    The second reading of the title is deliberate too. This course is
    unfinished, and so is the research behind it. Half the studies cited here
    are small. Several are contested. And the claim above has never been
    tested directly.
  </p>

  <h2>Where it starts, though</h2>
  <p>
    Not there. It starts with the part you already know. Re-reading a two-word
    reply more times than it deserved. Having the same thought at 2am you'd
    already had at 11pm. Shelving a decision because thinking about it more
    felt like doing something about it. The first four weeks are about that,
    out loud, in a room where everyone else is doing it too. The argument
    above doesn't get made properly until week 5, once you have a stack of
    your own examples to test it against.
  </p>

  <h2>What you will do</h2>
  <p>
    The semester pairs lectures with crits, and the crits are where most of it
    happens. Each one hands you a small provocation and turns you loose on it
    with the rest of the room. Overanalysing a text message in layers.
    Guessing the ending of something a classmate overthought. Making and
    eating a sandwich in complete silence. Running the same excessive rigour
    on a best-case scenario instead of a worst one. The lectures are held
    outdoors, and they supply the research the argument rests on. Every class
    opens with ten minutes of nothing.
  </p>

  <h2>What it isn't</h2>
  <p>
    Not a wellness course, and not therapy. The science here is real and
    cited, including the parts where the evidence is thinner than the internet
    suggests. You will be told which claims are findings and which are
    someone's hunch. The course's own central claim is labelled a hunch,
    because that is what it is. It also takes almost nothing seriously, which
    turns out to be the point rather than a tone choice.
  </p>

  <h2>Who it is for</h2>
  <p>
    Anyone who has re-read a two-word reply more times than it deserved, or
    shelved a decision because thinking about it more felt like progress. No
    background in psychology or philosophy needed. The only prerequisite is a
    mind willing to catch itself in the act. Nothing you share is marked, and
    you can pass on any activity without giving a reason.
  </p>

  <h2>Where to go next</h2>
  <CardGrid columns={2}>
    <Card title={sessionLabels.plural} href="/sessions/">
      <p>What happens in each crit, and what to bring.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="Lectures" href="/lectures/">
      <p>The outdoor lectures, and the research behind them.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="Assessment" href="/assessments/">
      <p>What is marked, what it is worth, and when it is due.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="People" href="/people/">
      <p>Who teaches the course and how to reach them.</p>
    </Card>
  </CardGrid>
</ContentLayout>

<style>
  .course-tags {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    margin-block: 0 2rem;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  .course-tags li {
    padding: 0.25rem 0.65rem;
    border-radius: 999px;
    color: var(--at-text-secondary);
    background: var(--at-bg-alt);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
  }
</style>
